WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.090
Film Courage: Any advice on screenwriting
books? Like what’s the best screenwriting

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.090
book you’ve read?

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:05.090
Zeke Zelker, Writer/Director/Producer of the
transmedia project BILLBOARD: I think Syd

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:11.020
Field is still probably the “Bible,” as
the first one I’ve ever read.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:20.170
I write everyday still to this day. I find
writing very enjoyable although it’s hellacious

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:26.730
at the same time. Because I know that’s
the only part of the process that is solely

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:32.870
me. Everything else is pretty much collaborative
and it’s give and take.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:37.720
When you are writing you don’t have to give
or take generally. I also take a long time.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:43.500
It’s funny, I will think about a script
for at least a year or two before I ever commit

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.390
anything to paper because what I do first
is I know the story I want to tell and then

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:55.610
I build out the characters and I have to build
out the characters so well that they can end

00:00:55.610 --> 00:01:00.850
up just talking to each other and then I can
literally vomit out a script in days is generally

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.220
how it works for me and then I do rewrites
from there.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:14.830
But also I only create characters that I will
want to play as an actor. Like my next project

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:27.000
is going to be a challenge for by two leads
in a big way. But that’s kind of why I love

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:35.520
writing and the thing is with writing it’s
all in the rewrite and I like to share what

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.520
I’m writing with people and get their honest
feedback. I don’t do it to people who are

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:45.170
going to be yes people and say “Oh this
is great!” I literally want real criticism

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:51.409
and also getting back to the whole entrepreneur
spirit of this stuff is that I’m so bold

00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:59.080
that I will hold live script readings of my
scripts for audience feedback. The audience

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:07.000
literally pays to go, I pay the actors, I
generally make money on my script readings.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.429
At the same time it’s very valuable because
I then hear what’s working, what is not

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:13.840
working and I also can get the audience’s
feedback.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.190
Again, all part of this story R &amp; D [Research
and Development] that I’ve been very much

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.480
involved in and how I create.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:27.590
I actually have four stages that I find you
need to create to be successful and this doesn’t

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:32.280
just go for film but goes for if you’re
a musician or an artist, you have a dance

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.280
company or whatever.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.600
It’s a matter of something that I’ve been
perfecting over the course of BILLBOARD [Zeke’s

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.780
transmedia cine experience project] and it’s
funny because you mentioned the whole NoFilmSchool

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.852
article and it’s literally the whole thing
I wrote in NoFilmSchool [referencing his post

00:02:42.852 --> 00:02:43.852
of March 2019 ‘Transmedia Pioneer: How I
Paved My Own Road as a Filmmaker’] of what

00:02:43.852 --> 00:02:49.520
I call the four stages of success for a creative
career and I’ve blogged about it as well.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.690
Writing is the most crucial part of this whole
thing. And if something is not working on

00:02:54.690 --> 00:03:02.250
set that’s also important that you sit down
and rewrite and make sure it will work.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:06.880
As I said before with BILLBOARD is that when
we got into post-production some elements

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:12.620
just didn’t work and so I had to rewrite.
And BILLBOARD is a very complex script to

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:16.050
write because not only did I have to worry
about the feature film I also had to worry

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:19.741
about how it would fit into the web series.
If I were to change one thing in the film,

00:03:19.741 --> 00:03:24.150
I had to make sure it worked for the web series.
It was very, very complex. And also our plan

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:30.500
with media some shots we shot we actually
had to do three different iterations of that

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.180
scene to get the entire scene.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:44.500
For instance we would have a newscaster speaking
on TV but on the screen and the newscast was

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:51.840
part of the billboard contest and then that
screen ended up being in another shot. So

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.989
just what you had to go through to make this
while thing happen. And that was a big challenge

00:03:55.989 --> 00:04:05.030
with the script. Literally having to realize
that one scene sometimes took three different

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.849
layers the scene cohesive.

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:14.050
Film Courage: You said you think about an
idea for a film for maybe one or two years

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:18.139
and then basically you vomit out the first
draft because you feel like you know the story

00:04:18.139 --> 00:04:23.110
and the characters so well. Do you give this
[first] draft some time before you go in for

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:24.949
the rewrite?

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:31.870
Zeke: Actually I do. I generally give it a
week or two and then I’ll reread it and

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:33.860
then from right there is when I start to make
notes.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:42.430
It’s funny because when people ask me to
read scripts I break out a pen and I don’t

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:47.960
know why but literally if it’s a formatting
issue or anything like that, if things are

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.419
not right in the first 10 pages I won’t
carry on.

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:52.639
Film Courage: You won’t? Will you tell them?

00:04:52.639 --> 00:04:57.270
Zeke: I’m like “I’m not engaged, rewrite
it. Your formatting is off, this…X…Y…and

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:02.240
Z and everything else.” Too many spelling
mistakes, I mean it’s crazy. I know it’s

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:09.280
sounds horrible but if you’re going to ask
me to take my time to read something make

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:17.170
sure it’s readable. It’s important, just
respectfully it’s important I think.

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:21.360
I’ve had people who are like “I’ve got
this great script.” And then I read it and

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:28.110
it’s not even in script format. It’s like
what the heck are you doing? I can’t even

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:34.050
read this. And people don’t understand either
that there is a formula for screenwriting

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:39.240
because as a producer now I know a page is
basically going to be a minute of screen time.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.909
If it’s a very black page it’s going to
be more than a minute (black meaning a lot

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:50.539
of action that is spelled out in a script).
And so I started thinking about budgets, I

00:05:50.539 --> 00:05:54.229
started thinking about all of these kind of
things if I’m reading a script whether I

00:05:54.229 --> 00:06:00.809
could produce it or what I would need to do
to make it happen. I’m always reading something

00:06:00.809 --> 00:06:05.441
as a producer first before I read it (I guess
that’s why I read so many script because

00:06:05.441 --> 00:06:12.699
I might want to produce it). My own scripts
though I’m very tough on myself. I just

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:15.139
write and rewrite and write and rewrite.

00:06:15.139 --> 00:06:20.610
Film Courage: How do you know when it’s
done?

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:31.509
Zeke: It’s not done until I’m editing
the picture. Because you still edit things

00:06:31.509 --> 00:06:39.490
so you are constantly editing. On location
you are editing generally because you might

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:44.379
use a certain line or something like that
so it’s funny or somebody says something

00:06:44.379 --> 00:06:49.560
wrong and all of the sudden you have to adapt
something to change. Yes you have to lock

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:55.439
in the script to be able to schedule everything
to have your production plans and so forth

00:06:55.439 --> 00:06:59.109
but at the same time the script is not really
locked until post-production.

